<p>Alice Dahners was born on December 2, 1885, to Thomas and Alice (Slattery) Kennedy at her parents’ homestead near Hazen, North Dakota. When she was three years old, her father drowned in the swollen Knife River during the spring breakup while bringing a lawyer across by boat to draw up the papers for ownership of their homestead. Her mother then moved her young family to Mandan; her youngest child was born three months after her husband’s death. Mrs. Kennedy supported her young family for 16 years as a dressmaker. Alice attended Mandan grade and high schools. In her last year of high school, she transferred to Valley City Normal School (today Valley City State University) to study to become a teacher. She recalled the topic of her June 1905 graduation speech as “The Experimental School at the Chicago University.”</p>

<p>She spent several years teaching in Finley and Bowbells, North Dakota. In Bowbells, she met Henry L. Dahners, who worked in real estate in Calgary, Alberta, Canada. She married Henry on April 8, 1912, and then moved to Calgary for four years. They were parents to three children: Lucile, Gwen, and Gregory.</p>

