<p>Their business grew to the point that they needed to move into a larger building, so they moved into the Lewis and Clark Hotel. In 1928, they moved to their final location at 306 West Main Street. With the expanded space, they added gifts and novelties, which were sold in the basement. In 1934, they added women’s ready-to-wear clothing and sold many wedding gowns. The business later became known as Dahners Music and Department Store. The store was sold to Bill Eckroth in May 1972 after the death of her husband.</p>

<p>Alice was a member of the Mandan Pioneer Daughters, which she helped organize. As a special project, she compiled histories of 120 pioneer mothers that are now on file at the State Historical Society. The histories are part of 3,000 compiled for the North Dakota Federated Women’s Club project from 1929 to 1932. Mrs. Dahners was proud of her membership in the American Women’s Club in Canada, even though she had left that country many years earlier. She was given a cherished life membership and Blue Bird pin for her 40-year affiliation. Every Sunday was a special day for the family, with a meal cooked by Mrs. Dahners, who was a trim, alert, well-dressed businesswoman as well as a loving wife and mother. Alice was also the hostess of frequent social gatherings, including a ladies’ bridge tournament in 1932 with a “Depression” theme. Guests came attired in makeshift clothing, and newspapers were used as tablecloths when refreshments were served. Alice Dahners passed away on Monday, September 7, 1964, and is buried at Mandan Union Cemetery.</p>
